{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbgZzEFn4kPYeTF9j3cunK","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"M1 全量 CodeQL 提取\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"5K SWE-smith repos data-flow extraction + connected vs gold statistics\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"repos_attempted\": 5000,\n7\t      \"repos_success\": 4720,\n8\t      \"repos_skipped_no_python\": 180,\n9\t      \"repos_timeout\": 100,\n10\t      \"codeql_timeout_pct\": 5.2,\n11\t      \"total_instances\": \"~24000\",\n12\t      \"instances_with_extra_connected\": \"~10100\",\n13\t      \"Z_pct\": 42.1,\n14\t      \"avg_connected_minus_gold\": 5.8,\n15\t      \"median_extraction_time_s\": 38.2,\n16\t      \"p95_extraction_time_s\": 210,\n17\t      \"by_repo_size\": {\n18\t        \"small_lt50\": {\n19\t          \"Z_pct\": 39.5,\n20\t          \"avg_extra\": 2.8\n21\t        },\n22\t        \"medium_50_200\": {\n23\t          \"Z_pct\": 54.2,\n24\t          \"avg_extra\": 10.5\n25\t        },\n26\t        \"large_gt200\": {\n27\t          \"Z_pct\": 18.3,\n28\t          \"avg_extra\": 9.2\n29\t        }\n30\t      }\n31\t    },\n32\t    \"tables\": [\n33\t      {\n34\t        \"name\": \"Table 2: CodeQL Extraction Statistics at Scale\",\n35\t        \"columns\": [\n36\t          \"Repo Size Bucket\",\n37\t          \"N Repos\",\n38\t          \"Z%\",\n39\t          \"Avg Extra Connected\",\n40\t          \"Timeout %\",\n41\t          \"Median Time (s)\"\n42\t        ],\n43\t        \"rows\": [\n44\t          [\n45\t            \"< 50 files\",\n46\t            \"~2800\",\n47\t            \"39.5%\",\n48\t            \"2.8\",\n49\t            \"2.1%\",\n50\t            \"18.5\"\n51\t          ],\n52\t          [\n53\t            \"50–200 files\",\n54\t            \"~1500\",\n55\t            \"54.2%\",\n56\t            \"10.5\",\n57\t            \"6.8%\",\n58\t            \"52.3\"\n59\t          ],\n60\t          [\n61\t            \"> 200 files\",\n62\t            \"~420\",\n63\t            \"18.3%\",\n64\t            \"9.2\",\n65\t            \"14.1%\",\n66\t            \"142.7\"\n67\t          ],\n68\t          [\n69\t            \"All\",\n70\t            \"~4720\",\n71\t            \"42.1%\",\n72\t            \"5.8\",\n73\t            \"5.2%\",\n74\t            \"38.2\"\n75\t          ]\n76\t        ]\n77\t      }\n78\t    ],\n79\t    \"figures\": [],\n80\t    \"analysis\": \"Z=42% at 5K scale confirms the 50-repo pilot was representative. The connected≠gold signal is robust and not an artifact of small-sample selection. Medium repos are the sweet spot for structural negative mining. Timeout at 5.2% is within budget and concentrated in large repos (>200 files), which are a minority of the dataset. This validates Claim 3 (scalability) and provides the foundation for training experiments.\\n\\n\\n50-repo pilot showed Z=40.8%, timeout=4%. At 5K scale, repo size distribution shifts slightly toward medium repos in SWE-smith, pushing Z marginally higher to ~42%. Timeout increases modestly to ~5% because the long tail of large repos grows — large repos have deeper call graphs that hit the 2-hop bound more often, but scope-bounding keeps it under 8%. The medium-repo Z dominance (~54%) persists because medium repos have enough inter-module structure to produce connected≠gold files but aren't so large that the gold set already covers most connected files. Small repos stay ~40% because their shallow call graphs limit reachability. The 42% Z means ~10K instances have structurally meaningful hard negatives available — sufficient training signal for contrastive learning.\\n\"\n81\t  },\n82\t  {\n83\t    \"name\": \"M2 全量 3-strategy 对比训练\",\n84\t    \"description\": \"random vs embedding_distance vs dataflow_aware, 3 seeds each, Qwen2.5-Coder-3B LoRA\",\n85\t    \"results\": {\n86\t      \"training_instances\": \"~12000\",\n87\t      \"epochs\": 3,\n88\t      \"batch_size\": 32,\n89\t      \"dataflow_s42\": {\n90\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0215,\n91\t        \"recall@5\": 0.862\n92\t      },\n93\t      \"dataflow_s123\": {\n94\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0248,\n95\t        \"recall@5\": 0.849\n96\t      },\n97\t      \"dataflow_s456\": {\n98\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0198,\n99\t        \"recall@5\": 0.871\n100\t      },\n101\t      \"dataflow_mean\": {\n102\t        \"val_loss\": 0.022,\n103\t        \"recall@5\": 0.861\n104\t      },\n105\t      \"random_s42\": {\n106\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0312,\n107\t        \"recall@5\": 0.792\n108\t      },\n109\t      \"random_s123\": {\n110\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0358,\n111\t        \"recall@5\": 0.778\n112\t      },\n113\t      \"random_s456\": {\n114\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0289,\n115\t        \"recall@5\": 0.801\n116\t      },\n117\t      \"random_mean\": {\n118\t        \"val_loss\": 0.032,\n119\t        \"recall@5\": 0.79\n120\t      },\n121\t      \"emb_dist_s42\": {\n122\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0485,\n123\t        \"recall@5\": 0.738\n124\t      },\n125\t      \"emb_dist_s123\": {\n126\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0512,\n127\t        \"recall@5\": 0.721\n128\t      },\n129\t      \"emb_dist_s456\": {\n130\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0468,\n131\t        \"recall@5\": 0.745\n132\t      },\n133\t      \"emb_dist_mean\": {\n134\t        \"val_loss\": 0.0488,\n135\t        \"recall@5\": 0.735\n136\t      },\n137\t      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_R5\": 0.071,\n138\t      \"delta_emb_vs_random_R5\": -0.055,\n139\t      \"val_loss_gap_dataflow_vs_random\": 0.01,\n140\t      \"paired_t_test_dataflow_vs_random_p\": 0.008,\n141\t      \"paired_t_test_emb_vs_random_p\": 0.012\n142\t    },\n143\t    \"tables\": [\n144\t      {\n145\t        \"name\": \"Table 3: Main Results — 3-Strategy Contrastive Training\",\n146\t        \"columns\": [\n147\t          \"Strategy\",\n148\t          \"Val Loss\",\n149\t          \"R@1\",\n150\t          \"R@5\",\n151\t          \"R@10\",\n152\t          \"Δ vs Random R@5\"\n153\t        ],\n154\t        \"rows\": [\n155\t          [\n156\t            \"Random (3 seeds)\",\n157\t            \"0.032 ± 0.004\",\n158\t            \"0.683 ± 0.018\",\n159\t            \"0.790 ± 0.012\",\n160\t            \"0.861 ± 0.009\",\n161\t            \"—\"\n162\t          ],\n163\t          [\n164\t            \"Embedding-Dist (3 seeds)\",\n165\t            \"0.049 ± 0.002\",\n166\t            \"0.621 ± 0.015\",\n167\t            \"0.735 ± 0.012\",\n168\t            \"0.808 ± 0.011\",\n169\t            \"-5.5pp\"\n170\t          ],\n171\t          [\n172\t            \"Dataflow-Aware (3 seeds)\",\n173\t            \"0.022 ± 0.003\",\n174\t            \"0.752 ± 0.016\",\n175\t            \"0.861 ± 0.011\",\n176\t            \"0.918 ± 0.008\",\n177\t            \"+7.1pp\"\n178\t          ]\n179\t        ]\n180\t      }\n181\t    ],\n182\t    \"figures\": [],\n183\t    \"analysis\": \"The 7.1pp R@5 gain is the headline result. Dataflow negatives produce a statistically significant improvement (p=0.008) over random, and the ordering dataflow > random > emb_dist is consistent across all 9 runs. Embedding-distance negatives harm by 5.5pp, consistent with MVP evidence but attenuated by larger training set. The val_loss convergence pattern confirms that dataflow training reaches a qualitatively different optimum — the loss curves diverge after epoch 1 and never reconverge. This is the core evidence for Claim 1.\\n\\n\\nAt full scale with ~10K instances having valid dataflow negatives, the model receives structurally informative contrastive signal that random negatives cannot provide. Random negatives are mostly easy (unrelated files) so the model converges quickly to a shallow optimum — val_loss ~0.032. Dataflow negatives force the model to distinguish between lexically similar but structurally disconnected files, pushing it past the random optimum to val_loss ~0.022. The 0.01 val_loss gap is smaller than the 0.02 threshold for \\\"different optimum\\\" but the R@5 gap of +7.1pp is the meaningful signal. Embedding-distance negatives are partially redundant with the encoder's own similarity — the gradient signal reinforces existing biases rather than correcting them, leading to a higher val_loss (~0.049) and lower R@5 (0.735). The MVP evidence already showed this pattern: emb_dist val_loss was 5x higher than random. At scale the gap narrows because more training data helps all strategies, but the ordering is preserved. Three seeds show low variance (std ~0.01 R@5) because the dataset is large enough to stabilize training. Statistical significance at p<0.01 because the effect is consistent across seeds and the evaluation set is ~2K+ instances.\\n\"\n184\t  },\n185\t  {\n186\t    \"name\": \"M3a SWE-bench Lite End-to-End Evaluation\",\n187\t    \"description\": \"3 strategies × best checkpoint, Qwen3.6-27B agent, 300 SWE-bench Lite instances\",\n188\t    \"results\": {\n189\t      \"n_instances\": 300,\n190\t      \"agent_model\": \"Qwen3.6-27B\",\n191\t      \"baseline_swerank_resolve_rate\": 0.312,\n192\t      \"dataflow_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.338,\n193\t      \"random_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.318,\n194\t      \"emb_dist_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.305,\n195\t      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_pp\": 2.0,\n196\t      \"delta_dataflow_vs_swerank_pp\": 2.6,\n197\t      \"delta_emb_vs_random_pp\": -1.3,\n198\t      \"swebench_lite_resolve_delta_pp\": 2.0,\n199\t      \"instances_where_retrieval_changed_outcome\": 18,\n200\t      \"instances_where_dataflow_uniquely_solved\": 9,\n201\t      \"instances_where_emb_dist_uniquely_failed\": 5\n202\t    },\n203\t    \"tables\": [\n204\t      {\n205\t        \"name\": \"Table 4: SWE-bench Lite End-to-End Results\",\n206\t        \"columns\": [\n207\t          \"Retriever\",\n208\t          \"Resolve Rate\",\n209\t          \"Δ vs Random\",\n210\t          \"Δ vs SweRank\"\n211\t        ],\n212\t        \"rows\": [\n213\t          [\n214\t            \"SweRank (baseline)\",\n215\t            \"31.2%\",\n216\t            \"-0.6pp\",\n217\t            \"—\"\n218\t          ],\n219\t          [\n220\t            \"Random negatives\",\n221\t            \"31.8%\",\n222\t            \"—\",\n223\t            \"+0.6pp\"\n224\t          ],\n225\t          [\n226\t            \"Embedding-Dist negatives\",\n227\t            \"30.5%\",\n228\t            \"-1.3pp\",\n229\t            \"-0.7pp\"\n230\t          ],\n231\t          [\n232\t            \"Dataflow-Aware negatives\",\n233\t            \"33.8%\",\n234\t            \"+2.0pp\",\n235\t            \"+2.6pp\"\n236\t          ]\n237\t        ]\n238\t      }\n239\t    ],\n240\t    \"figures\": [],\n241\t    \"analysis\": \"The 2.0pp end-to-end gain confirms Claim 1 transfers from retrieval metrics to downstream task performance. The gain is modest but expected given pipeline noise. The 9 uniquely solved instances provide strong qualitative evidence — in each case, the dataflow retriever surfaced a fix-relevant file that was structurally connected (2-hop reachable) but lexically dissimilar to the bug report. This is exactly the complementary signal dimension the paper claims.\\n\\n\\nEnd-to-end resolve rate amplification is inherently noisy — retrieval is one component in a multi-step pipeline (retrieval → context selection → patch generation → validation). A 7.1pp R@5 gain in retrieval translates to ~2pp resolve rate gain because: (1) many instances are solvable regardless of retrieval quality (easy fixes in obvious files), (2) some instances fail at patch generation regardless of correct retrieval, (3) the agent's own exploration partially compensates for retrieval misses. The 2.0pp gain is at the lower end of the best-tier range [1.5, 4.0] but is meaningful because SWE-bench Lite has high variance — 2pp on 300 instances is ~6 additional solves, which is detectable but not overwhelming. Embedding-distance retriever slightly harms resolve rate (-1.3pp) consistent with the retrieval-level findings but attenuated by agent compensation. The 18 instances where retrieval changed the outcome are the qualitative gold mine for the paper — they demonstrate that structural retrieval surfaces fix-relevant files the agent couldn't find on its own.\\n\"\n242\t  },\n243\t  {\n244\t    \"name\": \"M3b RepoBench-R Cross-File Retrieval\",\n245\t    \"description\": \"Retrieval-only evaluation on RepoBench-R, no LLM agent\",\n246\t    \"results\": {\n247\t      \"n_eval_instances\": \"~5000\",\n248\t      \"dataflow_R5\": 0.724,\n249\t      \"random_R5\": 0.688,\n250\t      \"emb_dist_R5\": 0.671,\n251\t      \"swerank_baseline_R5\": 0.695,\n252\t      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_pp\": 3.6,\n253\t      \"delta_dataflow_vs_swerank_pp\": 2.9,\n254\t      \"repobench_R_improvement_pp\": 3.6,\n255\t      \"MRR_dataflow\": 0.618,\n256\t      \"MRR_random\": 0.582,\n257\t      \"MRR_emb_dist\": 0.561\n258\t    },\n259\t    \"tables\": [\n260\t      {\n261\t        \"name\": \"Table 5: RepoBench-R Cross-File Retrieval\",\n262\t        \"columns\": [\n263\t          \"Model\",\n264\t          \"R@1\",\n265\t          \"R@5\",\n266\t          \"R@10\",\n267\t          \"MRR\"\n268\t        ],\n269\t        \"rows\": [\n270\t          [\n271\t            \"SweRank\",\n272\t            \"0.582\",\n273\t            \"0.695\",\n274\t            \"0.761\",\n275\t            \"0.591\"\n276\t          ],\n277\t          [\n278\t            \"Ours (Random neg)\",\n279\t            \"0.576\",\n280\t            \"0.688\",\n281\t            \"0.752\",\n282\t            \"0.582\"\n283\t          ],\n284\t          [\n285\t            \"Ours (Emb-Dist neg)\",\n286\t            \"0.551\",\n287\t            \"0.671\",\n288\t            \"0.738\",\n289\t            \"0.561\"\n290\t          ],\n291\t          [\n292\t            \"Ours (Dataflow neg)\",\n293\t            \"0.612\",\n294\t            \"0.724\",\n295\t            \"0.789\",\n296\t            \"0.618\"\n297\t          ]\n298\t        ]\n299\t      }\n300\t    ],\n301\t    \"figures\": [],\n302\t    \"analysis\": \"Cross-benchmark generalization validates that the structural signal is not overfit to SWE-bench distribution. The R@5 gain of 3.6pp on RepoBench-R, combined with 7.1pp on our eval set and 2.0pp end-to-end on SWE-bench Lite, provides a consistent picture across three evaluation settings. Notably, dataflow beats SweRank by 2.9pp despite SweRank being trained on a much larger corpus — quality of negatives matters more than quantity of training data.\\n\\n\\nRepoBench-R is a cross-file code completion benchmark where structural dependencies (imports, function calls) are central to relevance. Dataflow-trained retrievers have learned to internalize structural connectivity signals, so they naturally excel on tasks where the gold reference depends on inter-file relationships. The 3.6pp R@5 gain is smaller than the 7.1pp on our own eval set because RepoBench-R uses a different gold definition (next-line completion context) that doesn't perfectly align with our training objective (bug-fix relevance). The improvement is still in the best-tier range [2.0, 6.0] and demonstrates cross-task generalization — the structural signal learned from bug-fix training transfers to completion retrieval. Embedding-distance negatives again underperform random, though by a smaller margin (1.7pp) because RepoBench-R's distribution is less adversarial than SWE-bench.\\n\"\n303\t  },\n304\t  {\n305\t    \"name\": \"Embedding vs Random 深度分析\",\n306\t    \"description\": \"按 repo size/complexity 分层, 分析 embedding negatives 何时最有害\",\n307\t    \"results\": {\n308\t      \"strata\": {\n309\t        \"small_repos_lt50\": {\n310\t          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.032,\n311\t          \"n_instances\": \"~4800\"\n312\t        },\n313\t        \"medium_repos_50_200\": {\n314\t          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.071,\n315\t          \"n_instances\": \"~1800\"\n316\t        },\n317\t        \"large_repos_gt200\": {\n318\t          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.089,\n319\t          \"n_instances\": \"~400\"\n320\t        }\n321\t      },\n322\t      \"by_query_difficulty\": {\n323\t        \"easy_bm25_gt08\": {\n324\t          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.018\n325\t        },\n326\t        \"hard_bm25_lt04\": {\n327\t          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.092\n328\t        }\n329\t      },\n330\t      \"correlation_emb_harm_vs_repo_complexity\": 0.61,\n331\t      \"gradient_redundancy_metric\": 0.74\n332\t    },\n333\t    \"tables\": [\n334\t      {\n335\t        \"name\": \"Table 6: Embedding-Distance Harm by Repo Complexity\",\n336\t        \"columns\": [\n337\t          \"Stratum\",\n338\t          \"N\",\n339\t          \"Emb Δ R@5 vs Random\",\n340\t          \"Gradient Redundancy\"\n341\t        ],\n342\t        \"rows\": [\n343\t          [\n344\t            \"Small (< 50 files)\",\n345\t            \"~4800\",\n346\t            \"-3.2pp\",\n347\t            \"0.68\"\n348\t          ],\n349\t          [\n350\t            \"Medium (50–200)\",\n351\t            \"~1800\",\n352\t            \"-7.1pp\",\n353\t            \"0.76\"\n354\t          ],\n355\t          [\n356\t            \"Large (> 200)\",\n357\t            \"~400\",\n358\t            \"-8.9pp\",\n359\t            \"0.82\"\n360\t          ]\n361\t        ]\n362\t      },\n363\t      {\n364\t        \"name\": \"Table 7: Embedding-Distance Harm by Query Difficulty\",\n365\t        \"columns\": [\n366\t          \"Difficulty\",\n367\t          \"N\",\n368\t          \"Emb Δ R@5 vs Random\"\n369\t        ],\n370\t        \"rows\": [\n371\t          [\n372\t            \"Easy (BM25 R@5 > 0.8)\",\n373\t            \"~3500\",\n374\t            \"-1.8pp\"\n375\t          ],\n376\t          [\n377\t            \"Medium (0.4–0.8)\",\n378\t            \"~2500\",\n379\t            \"-5.6pp\"\n380\t          ],\n381\t          [\n382\t            \"Hard (< 0.4)\",\n383\t            \"~1000\",\n384\t            \"-9.2pp\"\n385\t          ]\n386\t        ]\n387\t      }\n388\t    ],\n389\t    \"figures\": [],\n390\t    \"analysis\": \"The stratified analysis provides the mechanistic explanation for Claim 2. Embedding-distance negatives harm most when the task is hard and the repo is large — precisely the setting where structural negatives provide the most value. The gradient redundancy metric is a novel diagnostic that could be useful for the community beyond this paper. This analysis strengthens the \\\"complementary signal\\\" narrative: embedding negatives fail because they operate in the same feature space the encoder already occupies; structural negatives succeed because they introduce an orthogonal signal dimension.\\n\\n\\nEmbedding-distance negatives are most harmful in larger repos and harder queries because: (1) in large repos, lexically similar files are more abundant, so embedding-distance selects negatives that are close in the encoder's existing feature space — this creates gradient signal that reinforces the encoder's current biases rather than teaching new discriminations. (2) For hard queries where BM25 already fails, the encoder's similarity surface is poorly calibrated, and embedding-distance negatives compound this miscalibration. In small repos with easy queries, the harm is muted because the retrieval task is simple enough that any reasonable negative strategy works. The gradient redundancy metric (0.74) quantifies that 74% of the gradient direction from embedding-distance negatives overlaps with the gradient from the current encoder's top-ranked non-gold files — confirming the \\\"redundant signal\\\" hypothesis from Claim 2.\\n\"\n391\t  },\n392\t  {\n393\t    \"name\": \"M1 Scalability Statistics\",\n394\t    \"description\": \"timeout rate, extraction time, coverage, connected-but-not-gold ratio by repo size\",\n395\t    \"results\": {\n396\t      \"total_repos\": 5000,\n397\t      \"timeout_rate_pct\": 5.2,\n398\t      \"mean_extraction_time_s\": 41.8,\n399\t      \"median_extraction_time_s\": 28.5,\n400\t      \"p95_extraction_time_s\": 198,\n401\t      \"p99_extraction_time_s\": 285,\n402\t      \"coverage_has_cg\": 0.96,\n403\t      \"coverage_has_df\": 0.91,\n404\t      \"Z_pct_overall\": 42.1,\n405\t      \"Z_by_language\": {\n406\t        \"python\": 43.8,\n407\t        \"javascript\": 38.2,\n408\t        \"typescript\": 41.5\n409\t      },\n410\t      \"extraction_cost_total_cpu_hours\": 82\n411\t    },\n412\t    \"tables\": [\n413\t      {\n414\t        \"name\": \"Table 8: CodeQL Scalability at 5K Repos\",\n415\t        \"columns\": [\n416\t          \"Metric\",\n417\t          \"Value\"\n418\t        ],\n419\t        \"rows\": [\n420\t          [\n421\t            \"Repos processed\",\n422\t            \"4720 / 5000 (94.4%)\"\n423\t          ],\n424\t          [\n425\t            \"Timeout rate\",\n426\t            \"5.2%\"\n427\t          ],\n428\t          [\n429\t            \"Median extraction time\",\n430\t            \"28.5s\"\n431\t          ],\n432\t          [\n433\t            \"P95 extraction time\",\n434\t            \"198s\"\n435\t          ],\n436\t          [\n437\t            \"Call-graph coverage\",\n438\t            \"96%\"\n439\t          ],\n440\t          [\n441\t            \"Dataflow coverage\",\n442\t            \"91%\"\n443\t          ],\n444\t          [\n445\t            \"Z% (connected ≠ gold)\",\n446\t            \"42.1%\"\n447\t          ],\n448\t          [\n449\t            \"Total CPU cost\",\n450\t            \"82 hours\"\n451\t          ]\n452\t        ]\n453\t      }\n454\t    ],\n455\t    \"figures\": [],\n456\t    \"analysis\": \"Validates Claim 3. CodeQL is practical at academic scale: 82 CPU-hours for 5K repos, <6% timeout, >90% coverage. The method is not specific to CodeQL — any static analysis producing a call-graph + dataflow graph would work. Cost is dominated by large repos, suggesting a simple repo-size filter could reduce timeout to <2% with minimal coverage loss.\\n\\n\\nThese are the same extraction as plan_001, reported from a scalability/engineering perspective. Timeout at 5.2% is driven almost entirely by repos with >200 files where the 2-hop call graph traversal explores a combinatorial number of paths. The scope-bounding (2-hop limit) keeps this manageable — without it, timeout would be >20%. Coverage is high (96% call-graph, 91% dataflow) because CodeQL's Python/JS/TS extractors are mature. The 5% coverage gap for dataflow is due to repos using dynamic features (eval, metaprogramming) that CodeQL cannot resolve statically. Total CPU cost of 82 hours for 5K repos means the method is practical for academic labs — a single 32-core machine processes the full dataset in ~3 hours wall time.\\n\"\n457\t  },\n458\t  {\n459\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Hop Count (1 vs 2 vs 3)\",\n460\t    \"description\": \"1-hop vs 2-hop vs 3-hop call-graph bounding\",\n461\t    \"results\": {\n462\t      \"hop_1\": {\n463\t        \"Z_pct\": 28.5,\n464\t        \"timeout_pct\": 1.2,\n465\t        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.831,\n466\t        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 3.2\n467\t      },\n468\t      \"hop_2\": {\n469\t        \"Z_pct\": 42.1,\n470\t        \"timeout_pct\": 5.2,\n471\t        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.861,\n472\t        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 8.7\n473\t      },\n474\t      \"hop_3\": {\n475\t        \"Z_pct\": 51.8,\n476\t        \"timeout_pct\": 18.5,\n477\t        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.852,\n478\t        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 24.1\n479\t      }\n480\t    },\n481\t    \"tables\": [\n482\t      {\n483\t        \"name\": \"Table 9: Hop Count Ablation\",\n484\t        \"columns\": [\n485\t          \"Hops\",\n486\t          \"Z%\",\n487\t          \"Timeout %\",\n488\t          \"Connected Set Size\",\n489\t          \"R@5\",\n490\t          \"Δ vs Random R@5\"\n491\t        ],\n492\t        \"rows\": [\n493\t          [\n494\t            \"1-hop\",\n495\t            \"28.5%\",\n496\t            \"1.2%\",\n497\t            \"3.2\",\n498\t            \"0.831\",\n499\t            \"+4.1pp\"\n500\t          ],\n501\t          [\n502\t            \"2-hop (ours)\",\n503\t            \"42.1%\",\n504\t            \"5.2%\",\n505\t            \"8.7\",\n506\t            \"0.861\",\n507\t            \"+7.1pp\"\n508\t          ],\n509\t          [\n510\t            \"3-hop\",\n511\t            \"51.8%\",\n512\t            \"18.5%\",\n513\t            \"24.1\",\n514\t            \"0.852\",\n515\t            \"+6.2pp\"\n516\t          ]\n517\t        ]\n518\t      }\n519\t    ],\n520\t    \"figures\": [],\n521\t    \"analysis\": \"2-hop is the clear sweet spot. This is an important ablation because it justifies a design choice that would otherwise seem arbitrary. The diminishing returns from 2→3 hop (while timeout triples) show that the method is not sensitive to the exact hop count — any reasonable scope-bounding works.\\n\\n\\n1-hop is too conservative — only direct callers/callees are connected, so Z% drops to 28.5% and the connected set is too small (3.2 files avg) to provide diverse negative mining. The retrieval gain is real but smaller (R@5=0.831) because the training signal has less structural diversity. 2-hop is the sweet spot — Z=42%, timeout=5.2%, and the connected set (8.7 files avg) is large enough for meaningful negatives without being so large that most files are \\\"connected\\\" (which would dilute the negative signal). 3-hop expands the connected set to 24 files on average, which improves Z to 52% but the larger connected set means many weakly-connected files are now excluded from the negative pool — some of these are genuinely good hard negatives. Additionally, timeout triples to 18.5% because 3-hop traversal is combinatorially expensive. The R@5 at 3-hop (0.852) is slightly worse than 2-hop (0.861) because the structural signal becomes noisier — too many files are \\\"connected\\\" and the negative/positive boundary becomes less informative.\\n\"\n522\t  },\n523\t  {\n524\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Training Data Size Scaling\",\n525\t    \"description\": \"1K vs 3K vs 5K training instances\",\n526\t    \"results\": {\n527\t      \"size_1K\": {\n528\t        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.821,\n529\t        \"random_R5\": 0.768,\n530\t        \"delta_pp\": 5.3\n531\t      },\n532\t      \"size_3K\": {\n533\t        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.848,\n534\t        \"random_R5\": 0.782,\n535\t        \"delta_pp\": 6.6\n536\t      },\n537\t      \"size_5K\": {\n538\t        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.861,\n539\t        \"random_R5\": 0.79,\n540\t        \"delta_pp\": 7.1\n541\t      },\n542\t      \"log_linear_fit_r2\": 0.97\n543\t    },\n544\t    \"tables\": [\n545\t      {\n546\t        \"name\": \"Table 10: Data Size Scaling\",\n547\t        \"columns\": [\n548\t          \"Training Size\",\n549\t          \"Dataflow R@5\",\n550\t          \"Random R@5\",\n551\t          \"Δ (pp)\",\n552\t          \"Instances w/ Structural Neg\"\n553\t        ],\n554\t        \"rows\": [\n555\t          [\n556\t            \"1K\",\n557\t            \"0.821\",\n558\t            \"0.768\",\n559\t            \"+5.3\",\n560\t            \"~420\"\n561\t          ],\n562\t          [\n563\t            \"3K\",\n564\t            \"0.848\",\n565\t            \"0.782\",\n566\t            \"+6.6\",\n567\t            \"~1260\"\n568\t          ],\n569\t          [\n570\t            \"5K\",\n571\t            \"0.861\",\n572\t            \"0.790\",\n573\t            \"+7.1\",\n574\t            \"~2100\"\n575\t          ]\n576\t        ]\n577\t      }\n578\t    ],\n579\t    \"figures\": [],\n580\t    \"analysis\": \"The log-linear scaling confirms that the method is not data-hungry — even 1K instances produce a meaningful 5.3pp gain. But the improvement from 1K→5K (+1.8pp) suggests that collecting more structural data is worthwhile. This analysis also serves as a practical guide for applying the method to new domains where structural data may be expensive to obtain.\\n\\n\\nBoth strategies benefit from more data, but the dataflow advantage grows because more data means more instances with valid structural negatives (Z~42% of instances have usable signal). At 1K instances, only ~420 have dataflow negatives, which limits the structural signal. At 5K, ~2100 instances contribute structural negatives, and the model has enough examples to learn the structural discrimination pattern. Random negatives also improve with scale but plateau faster because the signal from random negatives is less informative — more random negatives are just more easy negatives. The dataflow advantage scales roughly log-linearly with data size, suggesting that even more data (10K+) would continue to help but with diminishing returns. The delta grows from 5.3pp at 1K to 7.1pp at 5K, showing that the structural signal is data-efficient but benefits from scale.\\n\"\n581\t  },\n582\t  {\n583\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Fusion Head\",\n584\t    \"description\": \"embedding-only vs structural-features-only vs combined fusion head\",\n585\t    \"results\": {\n586\t      \"embedding_only\": {\n587\t        \"R5\": 0.79,\n588\t        \"note\": \"Equivalent to random-trained baseline\"\n589\t      },\n590\t      \"structural_only\": {\n591\t        \"R5\": 0.712,\n592\t        \"note\": \"Data-flow features alone, no embedding\"\n593\t      },\n594\t      \"combined\": {\n595\t        \"R5\": 0.861,\n596\t        \"note\": \"Full model\"\n597\t      },\n598\t      \"delta_combined_vs_embedding_pp\": 7.1,\n599\t      \"delta_combined_vs_structural_pp\": 14.9\n600\t    },\n601\t    \"tables\": [\n602\t      {\n603\t        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fusion Head Ablation\",\n604\t        \"columns\": [\n605\t          \"Configuration\",\n606\t          \"R@1\",\n607\t          \"R@5\",\n608\t          \"R@10\"\n609\t        ],\n610\t        \"rows\": [\n611\t          [\n612\t            \"Embedding only\",\n613\t            \"0.683\",\n614\t            \"0.790\",\n615\t            \"0.861\"\n616\t          ],\n617\t          [\n618\t            \"Structural features only\",\n619\t            \"0.598\",\n620\t            \"0.712\",\n621\t            \"0.778\"\n622\t          ],\n623\t          [\n624\t            \"Combined (ours)\",\n625\t            \"0.752\",\n626\t            \"0.861\",\n627\t            \"0.918\"\n628\t          ]\n629\t        ]\n630\t      }\n631\t    ],\n632\t    \"figures\": [],\n633\t    \"analysis\": \"This ablation decomposes the contribution of each signal source. The structural signal is complementary (7.1pp gain when added to embedding) but not sufficient alone (7.8pp below embedding-only). This supports the \\\"complementary signal dimension\\\" pillar of the paper's motivation and shows that the method enhances rather than replaces existing retrieval.\\n\\n\\nStructural features alone (is_connected, hop_distance, shared_callees) produce a weak retriever (R@5=0.712) because they are sparse and binary — a file is either connected or not, and the structural graph doesn't capture all relevance dimensions (e.g., a file can be relevant because it defines an error type mentioned in the bug, not because of dataflow). Embedding-only is the random baseline (R@5=0.790). The combined model achieves 0.861 because the structural features act as a complementary signal that helps the embedding focus on the right region of the file space — the structural features filter candidate files, and the embedding ranks within that filtered set. The 14.9pp gap between combined and structural-only confirms that embeddings remain essential; structure augments but doesn't replace text similarity. The 7.1pp gap between combined and embedding-only is the value of the structural signal — exactly matching the main result.\\n\"\n634\t  },\n635\t  {\n636\t    \"name\": \"Ablation: Distance-Weighted vs Binary\",\n637\t    \"description\": \"dataflow-distance weighted negatives vs binary connected/disconnected\",\n638\t    \"results\": {\n639\t      \"binary\": {\n640\t        \"R5\": 0.861\n641\t      },\n642\t      \"weighted_linear\": {\n643\t        \"R5\": 0.858\n644\t      },\n645\t      \"weighted_inverse\": {\n646\t        \"R5\": 0.864\n647\t      },\n648\t      \"delta_weighted_vs_binary_pp\": 0.3,\n649\t      \"p_value\": 0.42\n650\t    },\n651\t    \"tables\": [\n652\t      {\n653\t        \"name\": \"Table 12: Distance Weighting Ablation\",\n654\t        \"columns\": [\n655\t          \"Weighting\",\n656\t          \"R@5\",\n657\t          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n658\t        ],\n659\t        \"rows\": [\n660\t          [\n661\t            \"Binary (ours)\",\n662\t            \"0.861\",\n663\t            \"—\"\n664\t          ],\n665\t          [\n666\t            \"Linear distance\",\n667\t            \"0.858\",\n668\t            \"-0.3pp\"\n669\t          ],\n670\t          [\n671\t            \"Inverse distance\",\n672\t            \"0.864\",\n673\t            \"+0.3pp (n.s.)\"\n674\t          ]\n675\t        ]\n676\t      }\n677\t    ],\n678\t    \"figures\": [],\n679\t    \"analysis\": \"Simplicity validated — binary connected/disconnected is sufficient. This removes a potential reviewer concern about hyperparameter sensitivity in the distance weighting scheme.\\n\\n\\nThe binary connected/disconnected distinction captures most of the signal. Weighted variants (using hop-distance as a continuous feature) provide negligible additional gain because: (1) the structural graph is already scope-bounded to 2 hops, so the distance range is {0, 1, 2, ∞} — too coarse for meaningful weighting; (2) the key discrimination is between connected (any hop distance) and disconnected (∞), not between 1-hop and 2-hop connected files. The inverse-weighted variant marginally helps (+0.3pp) by upweighting negatives that are close to the connectivity boundary, but the effect is not statistically significant (p=0.42). This is a useful negative result — it shows the method's simplicity (binary signal) is a feature, not a limitation.\\n\"\n680\t  },\n681\t  {\n682\t    \"name\": \"Failure Analysis\",\n683\t    \"description\": \"50 SweRank/BM25 failures, categorize by whether data-flow helps\",\n684\t    \"results\": {\n685\t      \"failures_analyzed\": 50,\n686\t      \"categories\": {\n687\t        \"dataflow_helps_directly\": 14,\n688\t        \"dataflow_helps_partially\": 8,\n689\t        \"structural_irrelevant\": 18,\n690\t        \"gold_unreachable_by_any\": 10\n691\t      },\n692\t      \"dataflow_help_rate\": 0.44,\n693\t      \"common_failure_patterns\": [\n694\t        {\n695\t          \"pattern\": \"Gold file is utility/helper imported transitively\",\n696\t          \"count\": 11,\n697\t          \"dataflow_helps\": true\n698\t        },\n699\t        {\n700\t          \"pattern\": \"Gold file is test file with no structural connection\",\n701\t          \"count\": 9,\n702\t          \"dataflow_helps\": false\n703\t        },\n704\t        {\n705\t          \"pattern\": \"Bug is in configuration/data file not in call graph\",\n706\t          \"count\": 8,\n707\t          \"dataflow_helps\": false\n708\t        },\n709\t        {\n710\t          \"pattern\": \"Gold file shares API contract but no direct dataflow\",\n711\t          \"count\": 7,\n712\t          \"dataflow_helps\": true\n713\t        },\n714\t        {\n715\t          \"pattern\": \"Multiple valid fix locations, gold is one of several\",\n716\t          \"count\": 6,\n717\t          \"dataflow_helps\": \"partial\"\n718\t        }\n719\t      ]\n720\t    },\n721\t    \"tables\": [\n722\t      {\n723\t        \"name\": \"Table 13: Failure Analysis Categories\",\n724\t        \"columns\": [\n725\t          \"Category\",\n726\t          \"Count\",\n727\t          \"% of Failures\",\n728\t          \"Example\"\n729\t        ],\n730\t        \"rows\": [\n731\t          [\n732\t            \"Dataflow helps directly\",\n733\t            \"14\",\n734\t            \"28%\",\n735\t            \"Transitively imported utility\"\n736\t          ],\n737\t          [\n738\t            \"Dataflow helps partially\",\n739\t            \"8\",\n740\t            \"16%\",\n741\t            \"API contract without direct flow\"\n742\t          ],\n743\t          [\n744\t            \"Structural signal irrelevant\",\n745\t            \"18\",\n746\t            \"36%\",\n747\t            \"Test/config/data files\"\n748\t          ],\n749\t          [\n750\t            \"Unreachable by any method\",\n751\t            \"10\",\n752\t            \"20%\",\n753\t            \"New files in fix\"\n754\t          ]\n755\t        ]\n756\t      }\n757\t    ],\n758\t    \"figures\": [],\n759\t    \"analysis\": \"The failure analysis honestly characterizes the method's scope. Structural negatives help in 44% of failure cases — substantial but not universal. The ceiling analysis (20% unreachable) is important for reviewer expectations and frames future work: combining structural with other signals (test dependencies, git co-change history) could address the remaining 36%.\\n\\n\\n44% of baseline failures are helped by dataflow because the most common retrieval failure mode is missing transitively-imported utility files — these are structurally connected but lexically dissimilar to the bug context. The 18 structurally-irrelevant failures (36%) are cases where the gold file is a test, config, or data file that has no call-graph relationship to the bug — these represent the ceiling of structural methods. The 10 unreachable-by-any cases (20%) are instances where the gold patch touches files that no retrieval method could reasonably surface (e.g., documentation files, or files added in the fix that don't exist yet). The 44% help rate is consistent with Z=42% — roughly the same fraction of instances where structural signal exists.\\n\"\n760\t  },\n761\t  {\n762\t    \"name\": \"Qualitative Examples\",\n763\t    \"description\": \"5-10 cases where structural negatives changed retrieval outcome\",\n764\t    \"results\": {\n765\t      \"examples_selected\": 8,\n766\t      \"categories\": {\n767\t        \"transitively_connected_utility\": 3,\n768\t        \"shared_data_pipeline\": 2,\n769\t        \"cross_module_error_handler\": 2,\n770\t        \"api_contract_dependency\": 1\n771\t      },\n772\t      \"format\": \"Each example includes: bug description, gold file, baseline retrieval rank, dataflow retrieval rank, structural path (call chain), and explanation of why lexical similarity fails\"\n773\t    },\n774\t    \"tables\": [],\n775\t    \"figures\": [],\n776\t    \"analysis\": \"Qualitative examples make the abstract \\\"complementary signal\\\" claim concrete and intuitive for reviewers. The diversity of categories (utilities, pipelines, error handlers, API contracts) demonstrates that the structural signal is not limited to one pattern. These examples are selected for pedagogical value, not cherry-picked for maximum effect — the failure analysis (plan_011) provides the honest aggregate picture.\\n\\nThe 8 examples are selected to cover the major categories where structural negatives help. Each demonstrates the same pattern: the gold file is reachable via 1-2 hop call/dataflow edges but shares few lexical tokens with the bug context. Transitively-connected utilities are the most common (3 examples) because Python projects heavily use utility modules that are imported through intermediate files — BM25 and embedding-based methods miss these because the utility file's vocabulary is generic (e.g., \\\"parse\\\", \\\"validate\\\", \\\"convert\\\") while the bug context uses domain-specific terms. Cross-module error handlers (2 examples) illustrate a different pattern: the error handling code is structurally connected via exception propagation paths that only inter-procedural dataflow analysis captures.\\n\"\n777\t  }\n778\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0dedf751-1a16-4371-83cb-538d60ae5464","uuid":"a358ee13-26c4-4ae0-86f1-0ba2a2b51a8a","timestamp":"2026-05-12T20:57:50.145Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/faith-evidence-ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260512T204322/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"M1 全量 CodeQL 提取\",\n    \"description\": \"5K SWE-smith repos data-flow extraction + connected vs gold statistics\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"repos_attempted\": 5000,\n      \"repos_success\": 4720,\n      \"repos_skipped_no_python\": 180,\n      \"repos_timeout\": 100,\n      \"codeql_timeout_pct\": 5.2,\n      \"total_instances\": \"~24000\",\n      \"instances_with_extra_connected\": \"~10100\",\n      \"Z_pct\": 42.1,\n      \"avg_connected_minus_gold\": 5.8,\n      \"median_extraction_time_s\": 38.2,\n      \"p95_extraction_time_s\": 210,\n      \"by_repo_size\": {\n        \"small_lt50\": {\n          \"Z_pct\": 39.5,\n          \"avg_extra\": 2.8\n        },\n        \"medium_50_200\": {\n          \"Z_pct\": 54.2,\n          \"avg_extra\": 10.5\n        },\n        \"large_gt200\": {\n          \"Z_pct\": 18.3,\n          \"avg_extra\": 9.2\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 2: CodeQL Extraction Statistics at Scale\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Repo Size Bucket\",\n          \"N Repos\",\n          \"Z%\",\n          \"Avg Extra Connected\",\n          \"Timeout %\",\n          \"Median Time (s)\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"< 50 files\",\n            \"~2800\",\n            \"39.5%\",\n            \"2.8\",\n            \"2.1%\",\n            \"18.5\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"50–200 files\",\n            \"~1500\",\n            \"54.2%\",\n            \"10.5\",\n            \"6.8%\",\n            \"52.3\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"> 200 files\",\n            \"~420\",\n            \"18.3%\",\n            \"9.2\",\n            \"14.1%\",\n            \"142.7\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"All\",\n            \"~4720\",\n            \"42.1%\",\n            \"5.8\",\n            \"5.2%\",\n            \"38.2\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Z=42% at 5K scale confirms the 50-repo pilot was representative. The connected≠gold signal is robust and not an artifact of small-sample selection. Medium repos are the sweet spot for structural negative mining. Timeout at 5.2% is within budget and concentrated in large repos (>200 files), which are a minority of the dataset. This validates Claim 3 (scalability) and provides the foundation for training experiments.\\n\\n\\n50-repo pilot showed Z=40.8%, timeout=4%. At 5K scale, repo size distribution shifts slightly toward medium repos in SWE-smith, pushing Z marginally higher to ~42%. Timeout increases modestly to ~5% because the long tail of large repos grows — large repos have deeper call graphs that hit the 2-hop bound more often, but scope-bounding keeps it under 8%. The medium-repo Z dominance (~54%) persists because medium repos have enough inter-module structure to produce connected≠gold files but aren't so large that the gold set already covers most connected files. Small repos stay ~40% because their shallow call graphs limit reachability. The 42% Z means ~10K instances have structurally meaningful hard negatives available — sufficient training signal for contrastive learning.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"M2 全量 3-strategy 对比训练\",\n    \"description\": \"random vs embedding_distance vs dataflow_aware, 3 seeds each, Qwen2.5-Coder-3B LoRA\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"training_instances\": \"~12000\",\n      \"epochs\": 3,\n      \"batch_size\": 32,\n      \"dataflow_s42\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0215,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.862\n      },\n      \"dataflow_s123\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0248,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.849\n      },\n      \"dataflow_s456\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0198,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.871\n      },\n      \"dataflow_mean\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.022,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.861\n      },\n      \"random_s42\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0312,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.792\n      },\n      \"random_s123\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0358,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.778\n      },\n      \"random_s456\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0289,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.801\n      },\n      \"random_mean\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.032,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.79\n      },\n      \"emb_dist_s42\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0485,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.738\n      },\n      \"emb_dist_s123\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0512,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.721\n      },\n      \"emb_dist_s456\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0468,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.745\n      },\n      \"emb_dist_mean\": {\n        \"val_loss\": 0.0488,\n        \"recall@5\": 0.735\n      },\n      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_R5\": 0.071,\n      \"delta_emb_vs_random_R5\": -0.055,\n      \"val_loss_gap_dataflow_vs_random\": 0.01,\n      \"paired_t_test_dataflow_vs_random_p\": 0.008,\n      \"paired_t_test_emb_vs_random_p\": 0.012\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 3: Main Results — 3-Strategy Contrastive Training\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Strategy\",\n          \"Val Loss\",\n          \"R@1\",\n          \"R@5\",\n          \"R@10\",\n          \"Δ vs Random R@5\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Random (3 seeds)\",\n            \"0.032 ± 0.004\",\n            \"0.683 ± 0.018\",\n            \"0.790 ± 0.012\",\n            \"0.861 ± 0.009\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Embedding-Dist (3 seeds)\",\n            \"0.049 ± 0.002\",\n            \"0.621 ± 0.015\",\n            \"0.735 ± 0.012\",\n            \"0.808 ± 0.011\",\n            \"-5.5pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Dataflow-Aware (3 seeds)\",\n            \"0.022 ± 0.003\",\n            \"0.752 ± 0.016\",\n            \"0.861 ± 0.011\",\n            \"0.918 ± 0.008\",\n            \"+7.1pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 7.1pp R@5 gain is the headline result. Dataflow negatives produce a statistically significant improvement (p=0.008) over random, and the ordering dataflow > random > emb_dist is consistent across all 9 runs. Embedding-distance negatives harm by 5.5pp, consistent with MVP evidence but attenuated by larger training set. The val_loss convergence pattern confirms that dataflow training reaches a qualitatively different optimum — the loss curves diverge after epoch 1 and never reconverge. This is the core evidence for Claim 1.\\n\\n\\nAt full scale with ~10K instances having valid dataflow negatives, the model receives structurally informative contrastive signal that random negatives cannot provide. Random negatives are mostly easy (unrelated files) so the model converges quickly to a shallow optimum — val_loss ~0.032. Dataflow negatives force the model to distinguish between lexically similar but structurally disconnected files, pushing it past the random optimum to val_loss ~0.022. The 0.01 val_loss gap is smaller than the 0.02 threshold for \\\"different optimum\\\" but the R@5 gap of +7.1pp is the meaningful signal. Embedding-distance negatives are partially redundant with the encoder's own similarity — the gradient signal reinforces existing biases rather than correcting them, leading to a higher val_loss (~0.049) and lower R@5 (0.735). The MVP evidence already showed this pattern: emb_dist val_loss was 5x higher than random. At scale the gap narrows because more training data helps all strategies, but the ordering is preserved. Three seeds show low variance (std ~0.01 R@5) because the dataset is large enough to stabilize training. Statistical significance at p<0.01 because the effect is consistent across seeds and the evaluation set is ~2K+ instances.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"M3a SWE-bench Lite End-to-End Evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"3 strategies × best checkpoint, Qwen3.6-27B agent, 300 SWE-bench Lite instances\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_instances\": 300,\n      \"agent_model\": \"Qwen3.6-27B\",\n      \"baseline_swerank_resolve_rate\": 0.312,\n      \"dataflow_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.338,\n      \"random_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.318,\n      \"emb_dist_retriever_resolve_rate\": 0.305,\n      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_pp\": 2.0,\n      \"delta_dataflow_vs_swerank_pp\": 2.6,\n      \"delta_emb_vs_random_pp\": -1.3,\n      \"swebench_lite_resolve_delta_pp\": 2.0,\n      \"instances_where_retrieval_changed_outcome\": 18,\n      \"instances_where_dataflow_uniquely_solved\": 9,\n      \"instances_where_emb_dist_uniquely_failed\": 5\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 4: SWE-bench Lite End-to-End Results\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Retriever\",\n          \"Resolve Rate\",\n          \"Δ vs Random\",\n          \"Δ vs SweRank\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SweRank (baseline)\",\n            \"31.2%\",\n            \"-0.6pp\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Random negatives\",\n            \"31.8%\",\n            \"—\",\n            \"+0.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Embedding-Dist negatives\",\n            \"30.5%\",\n            \"-1.3pp\",\n            \"-0.7pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Dataflow-Aware negatives\",\n            \"33.8%\",\n            \"+2.0pp\",\n            \"+2.6pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The 2.0pp end-to-end gain confirms Claim 1 transfers from retrieval metrics to downstream task performance. The gain is modest but expected given pipeline noise. The 9 uniquely solved instances provide strong qualitative evidence — in each case, the dataflow retriever surfaced a fix-relevant file that was structurally connected (2-hop reachable) but lexically dissimilar to the bug report. This is exactly the complementary signal dimension the paper claims.\\n\\n\\nEnd-to-end resolve rate amplification is inherently noisy — retrieval is one component in a multi-step pipeline (retrieval → context selection → patch generation → validation). A 7.1pp R@5 gain in retrieval translates to ~2pp resolve rate gain because: (1) many instances are solvable regardless of retrieval quality (easy fixes in obvious files), (2) some instances fail at patch generation regardless of correct retrieval, (3) the agent's own exploration partially compensates for retrieval misses. The 2.0pp gain is at the lower end of the best-tier range [1.5, 4.0] but is meaningful because SWE-bench Lite has high variance — 2pp on 300 instances is ~6 additional solves, which is detectable but not overwhelming. Embedding-distance retriever slightly harms resolve rate (-1.3pp) consistent with the retrieval-level findings but attenuated by agent compensation. The 18 instances where retrieval changed the outcome are the qualitative gold mine for the paper — they demonstrate that structural retrieval surfaces fix-relevant files the agent couldn't find on its own.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"M3b RepoBench-R Cross-File Retrieval\",\n    \"description\": \"Retrieval-only evaluation on RepoBench-R, no LLM agent\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_eval_instances\": \"~5000\",\n      \"dataflow_R5\": 0.724,\n      \"random_R5\": 0.688,\n      \"emb_dist_R5\": 0.671,\n      \"swerank_baseline_R5\": 0.695,\n      \"delta_dataflow_vs_random_pp\": 3.6,\n      \"delta_dataflow_vs_swerank_pp\": 2.9,\n      \"repobench_R_improvement_pp\": 3.6,\n      \"MRR_dataflow\": 0.618,\n      \"MRR_random\": 0.582,\n      \"MRR_emb_dist\": 0.561\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 5: RepoBench-R Cross-File Retrieval\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Model\",\n          \"R@1\",\n          \"R@5\",\n          \"R@10\",\n          \"MRR\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"SweRank\",\n            \"0.582\",\n            \"0.695\",\n            \"0.761\",\n            \"0.591\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ours (Random neg)\",\n            \"0.576\",\n            \"0.688\",\n            \"0.752\",\n            \"0.582\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ours (Emb-Dist neg)\",\n            \"0.551\",\n            \"0.671\",\n            \"0.738\",\n            \"0.561\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Ours (Dataflow neg)\",\n            \"0.612\",\n            \"0.724\",\n            \"0.789\",\n            \"0.618\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Cross-benchmark generalization validates that the structural signal is not overfit to SWE-bench distribution. The R@5 gain of 3.6pp on RepoBench-R, combined with 7.1pp on our eval set and 2.0pp end-to-end on SWE-bench Lite, provides a consistent picture across three evaluation settings. Notably, dataflow beats SweRank by 2.9pp despite SweRank being trained on a much larger corpus — quality of negatives matters more than quantity of training data.\\n\\n\\nRepoBench-R is a cross-file code completion benchmark where structural dependencies (imports, function calls) are central to relevance. Dataflow-trained retrievers have learned to internalize structural connectivity signals, so they naturally excel on tasks where the gold reference depends on inter-file relationships. The 3.6pp R@5 gain is smaller than the 7.1pp on our own eval set because RepoBench-R uses a different gold definition (next-line completion context) that doesn't perfectly align with our training objective (bug-fix relevance). The improvement is still in the best-tier range [2.0, 6.0] and demonstrates cross-task generalization — the structural signal learned from bug-fix training transfers to completion retrieval. Embedding-distance negatives again underperform random, though by a smaller margin (1.7pp) because RepoBench-R's distribution is less adversarial than SWE-bench.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Embedding vs Random 深度分析\",\n    \"description\": \"按 repo size/complexity 分层, 分析 embedding negatives 何时最有害\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"strata\": {\n        \"small_repos_lt50\": {\n          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.032,\n          \"n_instances\": \"~4800\"\n        },\n        \"medium_repos_50_200\": {\n          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.071,\n          \"n_instances\": \"~1800\"\n        },\n        \"large_repos_gt200\": {\n          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.089,\n          \"n_instances\": \"~400\"\n        }\n      },\n      \"by_query_difficulty\": {\n        \"easy_bm25_gt08\": {\n          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.018\n        },\n        \"hard_bm25_lt04\": {\n          \"emb_vs_random_delta_R5\": -0.092\n        }\n      },\n      \"correlation_emb_harm_vs_repo_complexity\": 0.61,\n      \"gradient_redundancy_metric\": 0.74\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 6: Embedding-Distance Harm by Repo Complexity\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Stratum\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"Emb Δ R@5 vs Random\",\n          \"Gradient Redundancy\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Small (< 50 files)\",\n            \"~4800\",\n            \"-3.2pp\",\n            \"0.68\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Medium (50–200)\",\n            \"~1800\",\n            \"-7.1pp\",\n            \"0.76\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Large (> 200)\",\n            \"~400\",\n            \"-8.9pp\",\n            \"0.82\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 7: Embedding-Distance Harm by Query Difficulty\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Difficulty\",\n          \"N\",\n          \"Emb Δ R@5 vs Random\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Easy (BM25 R@5 > 0.8)\",\n            \"~3500\",\n            \"-1.8pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Medium (0.4–0.8)\",\n            \"~2500\",\n            \"-5.6pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Hard (< 0.4)\",\n            \"~1000\",\n            \"-9.2pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The stratified analysis provides the mechanistic explanation for Claim 2. Embedding-distance negatives harm most when the task is hard and the repo is large — precisely the setting where structural negatives provide the most value. The gradient redundancy metric is a novel diagnostic that could be useful for the community beyond this paper. This analysis strengthens the \\\"complementary signal\\\" narrative: embedding negatives fail because they operate in the same feature space the encoder already occupies; structural negatives succeed because they introduce an orthogonal signal dimension.\\n\\n\\nEmbedding-distance negatives are most harmful in larger repos and harder queries because: (1) in large repos, lexically similar files are more abundant, so embedding-distance selects negatives that are close in the encoder's existing feature space — this creates gradient signal that reinforces the encoder's current biases rather than teaching new discriminations. (2) For hard queries where BM25 already fails, the encoder's similarity surface is poorly calibrated, and embedding-distance negatives compound this miscalibration. In small repos with easy queries, the harm is muted because the retrieval task is simple enough that any reasonable negative strategy works. The gradient redundancy metric (0.74) quantifies that 74% of the gradient direction from embedding-distance negatives overlaps with the gradient from the current encoder's top-ranked non-gold files — confirming the \\\"redundant signal\\\" hypothesis from Claim 2.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"M1 Scalability Statistics\",\n    \"description\": \"timeout rate, extraction time, coverage, connected-but-not-gold ratio by repo size\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"total_repos\": 5000,\n      \"timeout_rate_pct\": 5.2,\n      \"mean_extraction_time_s\": 41.8,\n      \"median_extraction_time_s\": 28.5,\n      \"p95_extraction_time_s\": 198,\n      \"p99_extraction_time_s\": 285,\n      \"coverage_has_cg\": 0.96,\n      \"coverage_has_df\": 0.91,\n      \"Z_pct_overall\": 42.1,\n      \"Z_by_language\": {\n        \"python\": 43.8,\n        \"javascript\": 38.2,\n        \"typescript\": 41.5\n      },\n      \"extraction_cost_total_cpu_hours\": 82\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 8: CodeQL Scalability at 5K Repos\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Metric\",\n          \"Value\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Repos processed\",\n            \"4720 / 5000 (94.4%)\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Timeout rate\",\n            \"5.2%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Median extraction time\",\n            \"28.5s\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"P95 extraction time\",\n            \"198s\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Call-graph coverage\",\n            \"96%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Dataflow coverage\",\n            \"91%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Z% (connected ≠ gold)\",\n            \"42.1%\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Total CPU cost\",\n            \"82 hours\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Validates Claim 3. CodeQL is practical at academic scale: 82 CPU-hours for 5K repos, <6% timeout, >90% coverage. The method is not specific to CodeQL — any static analysis producing a call-graph + dataflow graph would work. Cost is dominated by large repos, suggesting a simple repo-size filter could reduce timeout to <2% with minimal coverage loss.\\n\\n\\nThese are the same extraction as plan_001, reported from a scalability/engineering perspective. Timeout at 5.2% is driven almost entirely by repos with >200 files where the 2-hop call graph traversal explores a combinatorial number of paths. The scope-bounding (2-hop limit) keeps this manageable — without it, timeout would be >20%. Coverage is high (96% call-graph, 91% dataflow) because CodeQL's Python/JS/TS extractors are mature. The 5% coverage gap for dataflow is due to repos using dynamic features (eval, metaprogramming) that CodeQL cannot resolve statically. Total CPU cost of 82 hours for 5K repos means the method is practical for academic labs — a single 32-core machine processes the full dataset in ~3 hours wall time.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Hop Count (1 vs 2 vs 3)\",\n    \"description\": \"1-hop vs 2-hop vs 3-hop call-graph bounding\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"hop_1\": {\n        \"Z_pct\": 28.5,\n        \"timeout_pct\": 1.2,\n        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.831,\n        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 3.2\n      },\n      \"hop_2\": {\n        \"Z_pct\": 42.1,\n        \"timeout_pct\": 5.2,\n        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.861,\n        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 8.7\n      },\n      \"hop_3\": {\n        \"Z_pct\": 51.8,\n        \"timeout_pct\": 18.5,\n        \"R5_dataflow\": 0.852,\n        \"connected_set_size_mean\": 24.1\n      }\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 9: Hop Count Ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Hops\",\n          \"Z%\",\n          \"Timeout %\",\n          \"Connected Set Size\",\n          \"R@5\",\n          \"Δ vs Random R@5\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1-hop\",\n            \"28.5%\",\n            \"1.2%\",\n            \"3.2\",\n            \"0.831\",\n            \"+4.1pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"2-hop (ours)\",\n            \"42.1%\",\n            \"5.2%\",\n            \"8.7\",\n            \"0.861\",\n            \"+7.1pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3-hop\",\n            \"51.8%\",\n            \"18.5%\",\n            \"24.1\",\n            \"0.852\",\n            \"+6.2pp\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"2-hop is the clear sweet spot. This is an important ablation because it justifies a design choice that would otherwise seem arbitrary. The diminishing returns from 2→3 hop (while timeout triples) show that the method is not sensitive to the exact hop count — any reasonable scope-bounding works.\\n\\n\\n1-hop is too conservative — only direct callers/callees are connected, so Z% drops to 28.5% and the connected set is too small (3.2 files avg) to provide diverse negative mining. The retrieval gain is real but smaller (R@5=0.831) because the training signal has less structural diversity. 2-hop is the sweet spot — Z=42%, timeout=5.2%, and the connected set (8.7 files avg) is large enough for meaningful negatives without being so large that most files are \\\"connected\\\" (which would dilute the negative signal). 3-hop expands the connected set to 24 files on average, which improves Z to 52% but the larger connected set means many weakly-connected files are now excluded from the negative pool — some of these are genuinely good hard negatives. Additionally, timeout triples to 18.5% because 3-hop traversal is combinatorially expensive. The R@5 at 3-hop (0.852) is slightly worse than 2-hop (0.861) because the structural signal becomes noisier — too many files are \\\"connected\\\" and the negative/positive boundary becomes less informative.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Training Data Size Scaling\",\n    \"description\": \"1K vs 3K vs 5K training instances\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"size_1K\": {\n        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.821,\n        \"random_R5\": 0.768,\n        \"delta_pp\": 5.3\n      },\n      \"size_3K\": {\n        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.848,\n        \"random_R5\": 0.782,\n        \"delta_pp\": 6.6\n      },\n      \"size_5K\": {\n        \"dataflow_R5\": 0.861,\n        \"random_R5\": 0.79,\n        \"delta_pp\": 7.1\n      },\n      \"log_linear_fit_r2\": 0.97\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 10: Data Size Scaling\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Training Size\",\n          \"Dataflow R@5\",\n          \"Random R@5\",\n          \"Δ (pp)\",\n          \"Instances w/ Structural Neg\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"1K\",\n            \"0.821\",\n            \"0.768\",\n            \"+5.3\",\n            \"~420\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"3K\",\n            \"0.848\",\n            \"0.782\",\n            \"+6.6\",\n            \"~1260\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"5K\",\n            \"0.861\",\n            \"0.790\",\n            \"+7.1\",\n            \"~2100\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The log-linear scaling confirms that the method is not data-hungry — even 1K instances produce a meaningful 5.3pp gain. But the improvement from 1K→5K (+1.8pp) suggests that collecting more structural data is worthwhile. This analysis also serves as a practical guide for applying the method to new domains where structural data may be expensive to obtain.\\n\\n\\nBoth strategies benefit from more data, but the dataflow advantage grows because more data means more instances with valid structural negatives (Z~42% of instances have usable signal). At 1K instances, only ~420 have dataflow negatives, which limits the structural signal. At 5K, ~2100 instances contribute structural negatives, and the model has enough examples to learn the structural discrimination pattern. Random negatives also improve with scale but plateau faster because the signal from random negatives is less informative — more random negatives are just more easy negatives. The dataflow advantage scales roughly log-linearly with data size, suggesting that even more data (10K+) would continue to help but with diminishing returns. The delta grows from 5.3pp at 1K to 7.1pp at 5K, showing that the structural signal is data-efficient but benefits from scale.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Fusion Head\",\n    \"description\": \"embedding-only vs structural-features-only vs combined fusion head\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"embedding_only\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.79,\n        \"note\": \"Equivalent to random-trained baseline\"\n      },\n      \"structural_only\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.712,\n        \"note\": \"Data-flow features alone, no embedding\"\n      },\n      \"combined\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.861,\n        \"note\": \"Full model\"\n      },\n      \"delta_combined_vs_embedding_pp\": 7.1,\n      \"delta_combined_vs_structural_pp\": 14.9\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 11: Fusion Head Ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Configuration\",\n          \"R@1\",\n          \"R@5\",\n          \"R@10\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Embedding only\",\n            \"0.683\",\n            \"0.790\",\n            \"0.861\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Structural features only\",\n            \"0.598\",\n            \"0.712\",\n            \"0.778\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Combined (ours)\",\n            \"0.752\",\n            \"0.861\",\n            \"0.918\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This ablation decomposes the contribution of each signal source. The structural signal is complementary (7.1pp gain when added to embedding) but not sufficient alone (7.8pp below embedding-only). This supports the \\\"complementary signal dimension\\\" pillar of the paper's motivation and shows that the method enhances rather than replaces existing retrieval.\\n\\n\\nStructural features alone (is_connected, hop_distance, shared_callees) produce a weak retriever (R@5=0.712) because they are sparse and binary — a file is either connected or not, and the structural graph doesn't capture all relevance dimensions (e.g., a file can be relevant because it defines an error type mentioned in the bug, not because of dataflow). Embedding-only is the random baseline (R@5=0.790). The combined model achieves 0.861 because the structural features act as a complementary signal that helps the embedding focus on the right region of the file space — the structural features filter candidate files, and the embedding ranks within that filtered set. The 14.9pp gap between combined and structural-only confirms that embeddings remain essential; structure augments but doesn't replace text similarity. The 7.1pp gap between combined and embedding-only is the value of the structural signal — exactly matching the main result.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Ablation: Distance-Weighted vs Binary\",\n    \"description\": \"dataflow-distance weighted negatives vs binary connected/disconnected\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"binary\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.861\n      },\n      \"weighted_linear\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.858\n      },\n      \"weighted_inverse\": {\n        \"R5\": 0.864\n      },\n      \"delta_weighted_vs_binary_pp\": 0.3,\n      \"p_value\": 0.42\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 12: Distance Weighting Ablation\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Weighting\",\n          \"R@5\",\n          \"Δ vs Binary\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Binary (ours)\",\n            \"0.861\",\n            \"—\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Linear distance\",\n            \"0.858\",\n            \"-0.3pp\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Inverse distance\",\n            \"0.864\",\n            \"+0.3pp (n.s.)\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Simplicity validated — binary connected/disconnected is sufficient. This removes a potential reviewer concern about hyperparameter sensitivity in the distance weighting scheme.\\n\\n\\nThe binary connected/disconnected distinction captures most of the signal. Weighted variants (using hop-distance as a continuous feature) provide negligible additional gain because: (1) the structural graph is already scope-bounded to 2 hops, so the distance range is {0, 1, 2, ∞} — too coarse for meaningful weighting; (2) the key discrimination is between connected (any hop distance) and disconnected (∞), not between 1-hop and 2-hop connected files. The inverse-weighted variant marginally helps (+0.3pp) by upweighting negatives that are close to the connectivity boundary, but the effect is not statistically significant (p=0.42). This is a useful negative result — it shows the method's simplicity (binary signal) is a feature, not a limitation.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Failure Analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"50 SweRank/BM25 failures, categorize by whether data-flow helps\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"failures_analyzed\": 50,\n      \"categories\": {\n        \"dataflow_helps_directly\": 14,\n        \"dataflow_helps_partially\": 8,\n        \"structural_irrelevant\": 18,\n        \"gold_unreachable_by_any\": 10\n      },\n      \"dataflow_help_rate\": 0.44,\n      \"common_failure_patterns\": [\n        {\n          \"pattern\": \"Gold file is utility/helper imported transitively\",\n          \"count\": 11,\n          \"dataflow_helps\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"pattern\": \"Gold file is test file with no structural connection\",\n          \"count\": 9,\n          \"dataflow_helps\": false\n        },\n        {\n          \"pattern\": \"Bug is in configuration/data file not in call graph\",\n          \"count\": 8,\n          \"dataflow_helps\": false\n        },\n        {\n          \"pattern\": \"Gold file shares API contract but no direct dataflow\",\n          \"count\": 7,\n          \"dataflow_helps\": true\n        },\n        {\n          \"pattern\": \"Multiple valid fix locations, gold is one of several\",\n          \"count\": 6,\n          \"dataflow_helps\": \"partial\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Table 13: Failure Analysis Categories\",\n        \"columns\": [\n          \"Category\",\n          \"Count\",\n          \"% of Failures\",\n          \"Example\"\n        ],\n        \"rows\": [\n          [\n            \"Dataflow helps directly\",\n            \"14\",\n            \"28%\",\n            \"Transitively imported utility\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Dataflow helps partially\",\n            \"8\",\n            \"16%\",\n            \"API contract without direct flow\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Structural signal irrelevant\",\n            \"18\",\n            \"36%\",\n            \"Test/config/data files\"\n          ],\n          [\n            \"Unreachable by any method\",\n            \"10\",\n            \"20%\",\n            \"New files in fix\"\n          ]\n        ]\n      }\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The failure analysis honestly characterizes the method's scope. Structural negatives help in 44% of failure cases — substantial but not universal. The ceiling analysis (20% unreachable) is important for reviewer expectations and frames future work: combining structural with other signals (test dependencies, git co-change history) could address the remaining 36%.\\n\\n\\n44% of baseline failures are helped by dataflow because the most common retrieval failure mode is missing transitively-imported utility files — these are structurally connected but lexically dissimilar to the bug context. The 18 structurally-irrelevant failures (36%) are cases where the gold file is a test, config, or data file that has no call-graph relationship to the bug — these represent the ceiling of structural methods. The 10 unreachable-by-any cases (20%) are instances where the gold patch touches files that no retrieval method could reasonably surface (e.g., documentation files, or files added in the fix that don't exist yet). The 44% help rate is consistent with Z=42% — roughly the same fraction of instances where structural signal exists.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"Qualitative Examples\",\n    \"description\": \"5-10 cases where structural negatives changed retrieval outcome\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"examples_selected\": 8,\n      \"categories\": {\n        \"transitively_connected_utility\": 3,\n        \"shared_data_pipeline\": 2,\n        \"cross_module_error_handler\": 2,\n        \"api_contract_dependency\": 1\n      },\n      \"format\": \"Each example includes: bug description, gold file, baseline retrieval rank, dataflow retrieval rank, structural path (call chain), and explanation of why lexical similarity fails\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Qualitative examples make the abstract \\\"complementary signal\\\" claim concrete and intuitive for reviewers. The diversity of categories (utilities, pipelines, error handlers, API contracts) demonstrates that the structural signal is not limited to one pattern. These examples are selected for pedagogical value, not cherry-picked for maximum effect — the failure analysis (plan_011) provides the honest aggregate picture.\\n\\nThe 8 examples are selected to cover the major categories where structural negatives help. Each demonstrates the same pattern: the gold file is reachable via 1-2 hop call/dataflow edges but shares few lexical tokens with the bug context. Transitively-connected utilities are the most common (3 examples) because Python projects heavily use utility modules that are imported through intermediate files — BM25 and embedding-based methods miss these because the utility file's vocabulary is generic (e.g., \\\"parse\\\", \\\"validate\\\", \\\"convert\\\") while the bug context uses domain-specific terms. Cross-module error handlers (2 examples) illustrate a different pattern: the error handling code is structurally connected via exception propagation paths that only inter-procedural dataflow analysis captures.\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":778,"startLine":1,"totalLines":778}}}
